============================================================================================
5.6 CONFLICT RESOLUTION - format term, all four combinations
============================================================================================
{faithfulness, ragas_faithfulness} x {pre-registered two-way, the
earlier six-term model}, on the identical 144-row core frame.
two-way  = C(format) + C(chunking) + C(format):C(chunking)
six-term = two-way + C(chunk_size) + C(generator) + C(corpus)
           (reproduces results/analysis/SUPERSEDED_anova_faithfulness_sixterm_legacyDV.csv)

[table saved -> thesis_tables\5_6_anova_sixterm_faithfulness.csv]

[table saved -> thesis_tables\5_6_anova_sixterm_ragas_faithfulness.csv]

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
format term - F, df, p, partial eta squared, all four combinations
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
                dv    model      F  df  resid_df      p  partial_eta_sq  format_sum_sq
      faithfulness  two-way 1.3158 2.0     135.0 0.2717          0.0191         0.0094
      faithfulness six-term 6.6818 2.0     130.0 0.0017          0.0932         0.0094
ragas_faithfulness  two-way 0.4605 2.0     135.0 0.6319          0.0068         0.0035
ragas_faithfulness six-term 1.9060 2.0     130.0 0.1528          0.0285         0.0035

[table saved -> thesis_tables\5_6_format_term_four_combinations.csv]

Identifying F(2,130)=6.68, p=0.0017 for the format term:
  faithfulness         x two-way   F=1.3158, df=(2,135), p=0.2717  -> does not match
  faithfulness         x six-term  F=6.6818, df=(2,130), p=0.0017  -> MATCH
  ragas_faithfulness   x two-way   F=0.4605, df=(2,135), p=0.6319  -> does not match
  ragas_faithfulness   x six-term  F=1.9060, df=(2,130), p=0.1528  -> does not match

  Six-term model on `faithfulness`, recomputed here: F=6.6818, p=0.0017
  results/analysis/SUPERSEDED_anova_faithfulness_sixterm_legacyDV.csv (the earlier run, renamed to flag it as superseded): F=6.6818, p=0.0017
  -> Recomputation matches the earlier file.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Decomposition - does the discrepancy come from the DV, the model spec, or both?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  format's own sum of squares, model spec held fixed, DV varied:
    two-way :  faithfulness SS_format=0.009434   ragas_faithfulness SS_format=0.003545
    six-term:  faithfulness SS_format=0.009434   ragas_faithfulness SS_format=0.003545

  core is a fully balanced/orthogonal design (3 format x 3 chunking x
  2 chunk_size x 2 generator x 4 corpus = 144, no empty cells), so under
  Type II SS, format's own sum of squares should be invariant to which
  other orthogonal factors are added to the model. Confirmed here:
  SS_format is IDENTICAL (to float precision) between the two-way and six-term models, for both DVs.

  Consequence: the MODEL SPEC changes SIGNIFICANCE only by shrinking the
  residual - adding C(generator) [eta_sq~0.57] and C(corpus) [eta_sq~0.75]
  removes variance from the error term (residual SS: faithfulness 0.4839 -> 0.0918), which inflates F for
  every remaining term including format, WITHOUT changing format's own
  effect. The DV changes the SIZE of the format effect itself: SS_format
  is 2.7x larger for faithfulness than for ragas_faithfulness, in
  both models - a real difference between the two constructs (5.5),
  independent of model spec.

  CONCLUSION: BOTH matter, for different, non-overlapping reasons.
    - Model spec is necessary for significance: even faithfulness's larger
      true effect is NOT significant under the two-way spec (F=1.3158, p=0.2717).
    - DV is necessary too: ragas_faithfulness stays non-significant even
      under the six-term spec (F=1.9060, p=0.1528) - the six-term model alone does not
      reproduce the original result on the RAGAS DV.
    - Only faithfulness UNDER the six-term spec reaches significance
      (F=6.6818, p=0.0017). Neither factor alone
      reproduces F(2,130)=6.68; both had to change together.

RESOLUTION: the pooled two-way model this thesis is pre-registered to
report (5.6 main loop above) finds no format effect on ragas_faithfulness
(F=0.4605, p=0.6319); that is the number to cite for H1a. The three-way and
per-corpus extensions below explain why: once corpus is modeled, format's
effect is real but reverses direction by corpus, which a model that
ignores corpus - two-way OR six-term, on either DV - cannot resolve.

============================================================================================
5.6 EXTENSION - THREE-WAY ANOVA (format x chunking x corpus)
============================================================================================
Adds corpus as a third factor, plus all two-way interactions and the
three-way interaction, to the pre-registered two-way model above.

